00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:11.760
SPEAKER: The Google
Cloud CLI, what is it?

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.830
The Google Cloud
Command Line Interface,

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:15.900
also known as the
Google Cloud CLI,

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.580
is a set of command
line tools that

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:20.910
are used to access the many
services on Google Cloud.

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:23.580
As part of the Google
Cloud SDK, the CLI

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:25.830
makes it easy for developers
and administrators

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:28.080
to manage their Cloud
resources and infrastructure

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:31.410
from their Linux, macOS,
or Windows terminal.

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:34.050
You can also access it without
having to download or install

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:36.960
anything by using Cloud
Shell, our in-browser shell

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:37.830
environment.

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:40.440
Several command line tools are
bundled within the Google Cloud

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:41.400
CLI.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:44.550
First, there's gcloud,
the primary CLI tool

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:46.710
used to customize your
local configuration,

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:49.890
manage authentication, and let
you access a majority of Google

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:51.107
Cloud resources.

00:00:51.107 --> 00:00:53.190
There are a lot of things
you can do with gcloud--

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:55.440
spin up a virtual machine
on Compute Engine,

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:59.610
create a database on Cloud SQL,
grant IAM roles and permissions

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:02.310
to users and service accounts,
manage your organization's

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:04.800
policies, and much more.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.620
Virtually anything you can do
in the Google Cloud console

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:09.420
by pointing and
clicking, you can also

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:11.580
do with a corresponding
gcloud command.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:13.860
Several other CLIs
and tools come

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:15.570
bundled within the
Google Cloud CLI,

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:18.630
including bq for interacting
with BigQuery from the command

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:19.290
line.

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:22.440
With bq, you can create and
manage BigQuery resources

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.720
like data sets,
jobs, and transfers,

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:27.420
and run queries on
your BigQuery tables.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.630
Next, there's gsutil for
accessing Cloud Storage

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:31.880
from the command line.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:33.630
If you're accessing
Cloud Storage buckets,

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:36.750
you'll use gsutil to
create, list, and delete

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:39.240
buckets, as well as upload,
download, and manage

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:41.110
the objects stored in them.

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:43.200
And what's more?
gcloud can run a number

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.310
of emulators that will generate
data for Bigtable, Datastore,

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:50.310
Firestore, Pub/Sub,
and Spanner for use

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:52.110
in your local
development environment.

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:56.550
All of these command line tools,
gcloud, bq, gsutil, and others,

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:59.220
can be used interactively
or programmatically

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:01.380
in any automated scripts.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:04.470
So how do you get started
using the Google Cloud CLI?

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:06.690
If you already have a
Google Cloud project,

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:09.300
you'll find it pre-installed
in Cloud Shell.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:12.780
For local development, it's
supported for Linux, macOS,

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:16.393
and Windows operating systems,
and requires Python to run.

00:02:16.393 --> 00:02:18.810
You can install it through
your operating system's package

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:19.480
manager.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:22.560
And once installed, the Google
Cloud CLI supports updates.

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:25.260
So you'll always have access to
the latest changes and features

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:27.270
available on Google Cloud.

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:29.310
You can find complete
installation instructions

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:32.400
at cloud.google.com/cli.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.320
For more on Google
Cloud CLI and how

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.000
you can get started
using it, check out

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.020
the links in the
description of this video.

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:39.060
Thanks for watching.

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:42.710
[MUSIC PLAYING]

